digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(6,0,0) Rendering devices light responsive requires additional circuit elements with mechanisms fundamentally different from biological systems."];
  "N11" [label="(0,0,0) If materials are mechanically hard, not biocompatible, and require non-biological mechanisms, then they are unsuitable for biomimetic systems."];
  "N12" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Current optoelectronic devices use inorganic materials with non-biological mechanisms, and such materials are unsuitable for biomimetic systems, so current devices have fundamental limitations for biological interfacing."];
  "N13" [label="(7,0,0) OMIECs can emulate ion-mediated mechanisms of biological synapses."];
  "N14" [label="(7,11,6) If materials use ion-based operating mechanisms akin to biology, then they are ideal for artificial neural circuits."];
  "N16" [label="(8,0,0) OMIECs are soft conjugated polymers with volumetric ionic/electronic transport and biocompatibility."];
  "N17" [label="(9,0,0) OECTs leverage OMIEC mixed conductivity to translate ionic biological information into amplified electronic signals in aqueous media."];
  "N21" [label="(11,0,0) OECTs exhibit both long-term and short-term plasticity behavior."];
  "N22" [label="(11,22,5) If devices exhibit synaptic plasticity behaviors, then they have high potential for integration as artificial synapses."];
  "N24" [label="(12,0,0) OMIECs have the ability to absorb light, which is crucial for neuromorphic vision but has yet to be exploited."];
  "N25" [label="(12,24,4) If materials can absorb light and conduct ions/electrons, then they can create light-responsive bioelectronic circuits."];
  "N28" [label="(14,0,0) These studies use light-sensitive materials in large-area gate electrodes, not leveraging OMIEC channel light sensitivity."];
  "N29" [label="(0,0,0) If devices rely on external light-sensitive components rather than intrinsic material properties, then they are difficult to operate, miniaturize, and integrate."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Human visual system processes inspire artificial visual systems through electronic devices."];
  "N31" [label="(15,0,0) The only miniaturized OECT optoelectronic synapse used P3HT:PCBM, which is incompatible with aqueous electrolytes and requires toxic ionic liquid."];
  "N32" [label="(15,10,12) If materials require toxic electrolytes and are incompatible with aqueous media, then they are unsuitable for biocompatible applications."];
  "N34" [label="(16,0,0) Current organic synaptic transistors process light at only one wavelength or restricted wavelength ranges."];
  "N35" [label="(0,0,0) If devices process only limited wavelengths, then they cannot achieve multispectral visual perception."];
  "N36" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Current devices process limited wavelengths, and if devices process limited wavelengths, then they lack multispectral capability, so current organic synaptic transistors have limited spectral functionality."];
  "N37" [label="(17,0,0) Visual memory and recognition rely on photoreceptors capturing visual information and afferent neurons transmitting electrical signals."];
  "N38" [label="(0,0,0) If biological visual systems use both optical sensing and electrical transmission, then biomimetic systems require multi-stimuli-responsive materials."];
  "N39" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: Biological vision uses optical and electrical modalities, and if biological systems use multiple modalities, then biomimetic systems need multi-stimuli materials, so mimicking human vision demands materials responsive to both light and electrical stimuli."];
  "N4" [label="(3,4,9) If biological systems inspire artificial systems, then nanomaterial-based electronic eyes and in-sensor processing devices can be developed."];
  "N40" [label="(18,0,0) Building biomimetic optoelectronic synapses requires multi-stimuli-responsive OMIECs with multispectral perception ability."];
  "N41" [label="(0,0,0) If materials have multi-stimuli responsiveness and multispectral perception, then they can surpass single-mode (electric or optical) drive functionality."];
  "N42" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Biomimetic synapses need multi-stimuli OMIECs with multispectral perception, and if materials have these properties, then they surpass single-mode drives, so developing such OMIECs is key to advanced optoelectronic synapses."];
  "N47" [label="(0,0,0) If materials combine ion-mediated operation, biocompatibility, intrinsic light sensitivity, and synaptic behaviors, then they can overcome current limitations in optoelectronic synapses."];
  "N50" [label="(0,0,0) If materials can overcome current limitations and enable multi-stimuli, multispectral operation in aqueous media, then they enable biomimetic optoelectronic synapses with superior functionality."];
  "N6" [label="(4,0,0) Optoelectronic synapses have been instrumental in neuromorphic devices for image decoding and artificial vision."];
  "N7" [label="(4,7,5) If synaptic devices are critical building blocks for neuromorphic computing, then optoelectronic synaptic devices enable neuromorphic vision."];
  "N9" [label="(5,0,0) Most optoelectronic devices in neural networks use inorganic materials that are mechanically hard, not biocompatible, and lack functionalities."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper aims to develop and evaluate a single-component light-absorbing n-type OMIEC, p(C2F-z), as the channel of an aqueous-electrolyte organic electrochemical transistor to function as an ion-tunable optoelectrochemical synapse, converting electrical inputs and multispectral UV–visible–NIR light into synaptic-like plastic responses via light-induced ionic doping for integrated image sensing, preprocessing, memory, and spatiotemporal neuromorphic computing.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When current artificial-vision synapse technologies have biological-interface, integration, spectral, and modality limitations, and OMIEC/OECT materials offer ion-mediated, aqueous, light-absorbing, and synaptic properties, it is scientifically motivated to develop and evaluate an integrated OMIEC-based optoelectrochemical synapse."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Human visual-system processes motivate the development of bio-inspired artificial visual systems, including nanomaterial-based electronic eyes and in-sensor processing devices."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) OECTs using OMIEC mixed ionic/electronic conductivity are suitable platforms to translate ionic biological information into amplified electronic signals in aqueous bioelectronic settings."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) OECT optoelectronic synapse designs that rely on large-area external light-sensitive gate materials, rather than intrinsic OMIEC channel light sensitivity, face operation, miniaturization, and integration challenges."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) OMIEC/OECT platforms combine properties relevant to next-generation optoelectronic synapses: ion-mediated biological operation, aqueous bioelectronic compatibility, synaptic plasticity behavior, and intrinsic light absorption."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) A new OMIEC-based approach is motivated for biomimetic optoelectronic synapses that require multi-stimuli responsiveness, multispectral perception, and improved compatibility with aqueous bioelectronic operation."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Optoelectronic synaptic devices can support neuromorphic vision functions such as image decoding and artificial vision, rather than being established as essential for all artificial vision systems."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because OMIECs emulate ion-mediated biological synaptic mechanisms, they are well suited as candidate materials for biomimetic artificial neural circuits."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because OECTs exhibit long-term and short-term synaptic plasticity behaviors, they have high potential for integration as artificial synaptic devices."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) OMIECs' light absorption and mixed ionic/electronic transport motivate their use in light-responsive bioelectronic circuits and optoelectronic applications."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) The reported miniaturized OECT optoelectronic synapse based on P3HT:PCBM has aqueous-electrolyte incompatibility and toxic-ionic-liquid limitations for biocompatible applications."];
  "N9" -> "N12" [label="induction-case"];
  "N10" -> "N12" [label="induction-case"];
